          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-jane-eyre-41b2d0196e9af2ce\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Jane directly avows that she loves Rochester deeply. The clause primarily states her emotional condition and grounds her refusal to flatter him; it does not itself undertake a commitment. The intensifiers “most dearly” and “far too dearly” indicate degree and motivate honesty, but provide no independent evidence of expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The surrounding engagement gives the avowal relational significance, but that context does not make this particular clause a commitment-enacting performance.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Jane directly addresses Rochester within their conversation: “I love you most dearly.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Jane uses the avowal as part of a corrective argument: because she loves Rochester too dearly to flatter him, she insists that they recognize each other honestly rather than adopt his idealizing, ornamental vision of marriage. Her love is sincere and emphatic, but the immediate force is an avowal supporting candour, not a reflexive outburst or a new relational undertaking.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I don’t call you handsome, sir, though I love you most dearly”\",\"supports\":\"The concessive construction explicitly presents Jane’s love as true despite her refusal to praise Rochester’s appearance, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“far too dearly to flatter you. Don’t flatter me.”\",\"supports\":\"Jane invokes the depth of her love as a reason for reciprocal honesty. This argumentative function supports an avowal rather than an expressive discharge and does not independently enact a commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The exchange occurs amid Rochester’s plans to dress Jane in jewels and fine clothes, which she rejects as making her cease to be herself.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal serves Jane’s resistance to idealization and her demand to be known authentically; this contextual role is adequately represented by T without O.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-form explicit “I love you” in Chapter XXIV of Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre.\",\"supports\":\"This corroborates identification of the classified core occurrence and its canonical location.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Its argumentative use, emotional intensity, and placement within an engagement scene are contextual features rather than missing dimensions of core force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
